        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "33d4a128-a64f-54a7-9a0e-7f37d6bb2ec3",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Kanoldt\n- Art movements and genres: Magic realism, Magical realism, New Objectivity, Fabulism\n- Topics: Narrative fiction, Literature, Painting, Film, Theatre\n\nFacts:\n- Alexander Kanoldt was a German magic realist painter born on 29 September 1881 and died on 24 January 1939.\n- Alexander Kanoldt was one of the artists of the New Objectivity.\n- Magic realism is a genre of narrative fiction and, more broadly, a mode of art including literature, painting, film, and theatre.\n- Magic realism presents a primarily realistic view of the world while also adding or revealing magical elements.\n- Magic realism is sometimes called fabulism in reference to the conventions of fables, myths, and allegory.\n- The term \"magical realism\" often refers specifically to fiction and literature in which magic or the supernatural appears in an otherwise real-world or mundane setting.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "e68e67c1-5fc1-5b1e-8ed4-6a3ccde1d3b6",
        "text": "Page: Alexander Kanoldt. Sentence: Alexander Kanoldt (29 September 1881 – 24 January 1939) was a German magic realist painter and one of the artists of the New Objectivity.\nPage: Magic realism. Sentence: Magical realism, magic realism, or marvelous realism is a genre of narrative fiction and, more broadly, art (literature, painting, film, theatre, etc.) that, while encompassing a range of subtly different concepts, expresses a primarily realistic view of the real world while also adding or revealing magical elements.\nPage: Magic realism. Sentence: It is sometimes called fabulism, in reference to the conventions of fables, myths, and allegory.\nPage: Magic realism. Sentence: \"Magical realism\", perhaps the most common term, often refers to fiction and literature in particular, with magic or the supernatural presented in an otherwise real-world or mundane setting.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
